  "body": "All three proposals executed as expected, but the question is whether the properties reveal genuine divergences between rule and code.\n\n**Property 1 (recovery/float rejection): STANDS, but not a divergence.** The rule states 'A candidate that parses as strict JSON... is scoreable; one that does not (a float, for instance) is passed over' [9cda97b84b710fb0]. The code rejects floats at parse time via `parse_float=_refuse_float` in `_loads_last_wins` [d2486046666b6d92]. The execution confirmed 'unchanged' [1fde7e9439787b00]. This is not a rule-code divergence; the mechanism (parse-time vs. post-filter) was underspecified in the rule but the behavior matches. Proposer correctly identified the mechanism but this is not a failure.\n\n**Property 2 (span-occurs/relaxation returns): STANDS, genuine divergence.** The rule promises 'verbatim or NOT_IN_DOCUMENT' with 'no relaxation' [9cda97b84b710fb0]. The code returns 'case_insensitive' or 'date_range_completion' when those relaxations are in the tuple [d2486046666b6d92, line `_span_occurs`]. Execution showed 'moved' from NOT_IN_DOCUMENT to verbatim [1fde7e9439787b00]. This matters: the rule's contract is narrower than the code's behavior. A proposer should test whether these relaxation returns leak into the public `conformance.kernel.span-occurs` verdict or are internal-only.\n\n**Property 3 (grounding/case relaxation): STANDS, genuine divergence.** The rule states 'no relaxation, value-in-span checked' [9cda97b84b710fb0]. The code uses `.casefold()` for case-insensitive comparison: `_normalise_whitespace(fields[\"value\"]).casefold() not in _normalise_whitespace(fields[\"span\"]).casefold()` [d2486046666b6d92, line `_grounding_kernel`]. Execution showed 'unchanged' (both GROUNDED) [1fde7e9439787b00]. This matters: 'no relaxation' in the rule contradicts case-insensitive matching in the code. A proposer should test whether this case relaxation applies to all grounding checks or only specific transforms.\n\nTwo of